# The geocoder of the US Census Bureau, which turns an address in the United States into
# latitude and longitude. The field and its type are written by hand over its
# onelineaddress operation.

extend type Query {
	"""
	Finds an address in the United States with the US Census Bureau geocoder and returns its latitude and
	longitude, best match first, for example 1437 Bannock St, Denver, CO. Use it to turn an owner's address, or any
	other address, into the point that the weather, earthquake, wildlife and fuel station fields of other services
	take. The list is empty when the geocoder cannot find the address. One call to the geocoder.
	"""
	censusGeocodeAddress(
		"""
		The address on one line, with the street, the city and the state, at most 100 characters, for example 1437
		Bannock St, Denver, CO. Give the state: without it, 110 W. Liberty St., Madison matched an address in
		Madison, Connecticut.
		"""
		address: String!
	): [CensusAddressMatch!]!
}

"""
An address that the US Census Bureau geocoder found, with its latitude and longitude. The geocoder places the
point by interpolating along the range of house numbers of the street segment, so the point is an estimate
within a few dozen metres.
"""
type CensusAddressMatch {
	"The address as the geocoder understood it, in capitals, for example 1437 BANNOCK ST, DENVER, CO, 80202."
	matchedAddress: String!
	"The latitude of the address in degrees, for example 39.739040 in Denver."
	latitude: Float!
	"The longitude of the address in degrees, negative in the United States, for example -104.990312 in Denver."
	longitude: Float!
	"The city, in capitals."
	city: String
	"The two-letter code of the state, for example CO."
	state: String
	"The five-digit ZIP code."
	zipCode: String
	"The first house number of the street segment that the point was interpolated along, for example 1401."
	fromHouseNumber: String
	"The last house number of that street segment, for example 1499."
	toHouseNumber: String
}
